    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sand goes first, then sealer. This is one of the few sequencing questions in hardscaping with a single correct answer, because polymeric sand needs to bond to bare paver faces — and a sealer applied first is specifically designed to stop anything bonding to them.
        </p>

        <div id="the-order" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Correct Order and Why It Is Not Negotiable
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand develops its holding power by gripping the vertical faces of the pavers on either side of the joint. Those faces need to be clean, bare and slightly porous for that grip to develop. A sealer applied beforehand coats them with exactly the kind of smooth, non-porous film that prevents adhesion — which is, after all, what a sealer is for.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Order</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Result</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand first, then seal (correct)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand grips bare paver faces; sealer protects the finished surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seal first, then sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand cannot bond to coated joint walls; joints fail early</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seal, sand, seal again</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The first sealer still blocks the bond; the extra coat changes nothing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand and seal the same week</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Traps curing moisture; produces a milky haze</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Sequence in One Line</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sand, wait about 30 days, clean, dry, then seal.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The waiting period between the two operations matters as much as the order. Polymeric sand keeps releasing moisture for weeks after it feels hard, and sealing before it has finished traps that moisture under the film as permanent cloudiness.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="seal-first-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Goes Wrong If You Seal First
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Joint Walls Are Coated</h3>

          <p>
            Sealer does not stop neatly at the paver edge. It runs down into the joints and coats the vertical faces, and on a film-forming sealer that leaves a smooth, closed surface. Polymeric sand installed into that joint cures into a plug that is sitting between two slippery walls rather than keyed into two porous ones. It has no meaningful grip, and traffic and water lift it out as a strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sand Will Not Sweep Cleanly Off a Sealed Surface</h3>

          <p>
            There is a practical problem alongside the technical one. A sealed paver surface, particularly a wet-look or gloss finish, holds fine sand in a way bare stone does not. Getting every trace off before watering becomes considerably harder, and any sand left behind cures onto the sealer as haze.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Cannot Simply Reseal Over It</h3>

          <p>
            The instinct on discovering the error is to add another coat of sealer to lock things down. It does not work: the original sealer is still on the joint walls, still preventing the bond, and adding film on top does nothing for the joint underneath. It also risks the layering problems that come with building up sealer coats.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixing It Means Stripping</h3>

          <p>
            This is why the mistake matters. Putting it right means chemically stripping the sealer off the pavers and out of the joints, cleaning thoroughly, letting everything dry, and then starting the sequence again correctly. That is a significant job with real risk to the paver surface, which is a poor trade for having saved a month.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="already-sealed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Common Real Situation: Pavers That Are Already Sealed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people asking this question are not planning a new installation. They have a sealed patio, the joints have eroded, and they want to put sand back. That is a genuinely different problem and it has better answers than a full strip.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option One: Strip, Sand, Reseal</h3>

          <p>
            The thorough approach and the one that gives the best long-term result. Strip the sealer, install polymeric sand properly into bare joints, wait for the cure, then reseal the whole patio. It is the most work but it puts you back on the correct sequence with joints that will actually hold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option Two: A Joint Stabilising Sealer</h3>

          <p>
            Purpose-made joint stabilising sealers are formulated to penetrate loose joint sand and bind it in place, and they are designed for exactly this situation. They are not as strong as properly installed polymeric sand, but they need no stripping and they are a reasonable answer for a patio with light traffic and joints that are eroded rather than empty.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option Three: Clean the Joints Only</h3>

          <p>
            A middle path. Rather than stripping the entire surface, clean out the joints thoroughly — pressure washing along the joints, or a powered joint cleaner — which removes old sand and much of the sealer film from the joint walls specifically. It is imperfect, since some sealer remains, but it recovers a substantial part of the bond and is far less work than a full strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which to choose depends on how the joints have failed. Empty joints on a well-used driveway justify the full strip and proper polymeric sand. Slightly eroded joints on a lightly used patio are a good candidate for a stabilising sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How the Type of Sealer Affects the Answer
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Effect on Joint Bond</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Film-forming, gloss or wet look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Severe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coats joint walls with a closed film; worst case for bonding</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Film-forming, matte or natural</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Severe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same mechanism, less visible on the surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sits in the pore structure rather than as a film; some grip may remain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint stabilising sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">By design</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Formulated to bind joint sand rather than exclude it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers are the least damaging to a subsequent sand installation, because they line the pore structure rather than forming a continuous film across the surface. Grip is reduced rather than eliminated. It is still the wrong order, but the outcome is less catastrophic than with a gloss film-forming product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Stabilising Sealers Are the Exception</h3>

          <p>
            These deserve separating out because they invert the logic. They are formulated to soak into joint sand and bind it, so they are applied deliberately after sand and are the one product designed to interact with the joint rather than seal it off. If your situation is loose sand in an already-sealed patio, this is the category to look at rather than trying to force polymeric sand into a coated joint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="full-sequence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Full Sequence for a New or Re-Sanded Patio
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clean the pavers and clear the joints</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Remove old sand, weeds and debris. Joints need to reach the manufacturer minimum depth, usually an inch to an inch and a half.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Let everything dry completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A full dry day after any washing, and longer in cool or humid weather. Joints hold moisture long after the surface looks dry.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Install polymeric sand in stages</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sweep in, consolidate by tamping or vibrating, top up, and repeat until joints sit about an eighth of an inch below the paver surface.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear the paver faces completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Broom, then a leaf blower at a shallow angle. This is the step that prevents permanent haze.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Water in light passes</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Shower setting, several passes, saturating the joint through its full depth. Milky runoff means binder is leaving the joints.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Keep traffic off, then wait about 30 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Foot traffic at 24 hours, vehicles at 24 to 48, and a full month before sealing.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clean again and allow a full dry day</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Remove dirt and efflorescence before sealing, then let the surface and joints dry completely.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Apply the sealer</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">In suitable weather within the product’s temperature range, with a dry window afterwards.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two points in that sequence catch people out. The first is that cleaning before sealing re-wets the joints, so it needs its own drying day rather than being done the same morning. The second is that the 30-day wait is not padding — polymeric sand keeps releasing moisture long after it feels solid, and a sealer film traps it as haze.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-vs-existing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. New Installations and Existing Patios Are Different Problems
          </h2>

          <p>
            The order question has one answer, but the practical situation you are in changes what you should actually do next.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What To Do</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Difficulty</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New paver installation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand, wait 30 days, then seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Straightforward</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unsealed patio, joints eroded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clear joints, re-sand, wait, then seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Straightforward</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed patio, joints slightly eroded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joint stabilising sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed patio, joints empty</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean joints out or strip, then re-sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to hard</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed patio, sand already failed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip sealer, re-sand properly, reseal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed too soon, hazy finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip sealer, allow full cure, reseal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Easy Case People Miss</h3>

          <p>
            A sealed patio with joints that have eroded slightly but are not empty is the most common situation and the one with the easiest answer. A joint stabilising sealer soaks into the remaining sand and binds it, needs no stripping, and takes an afternoon. People frequently skip past this because they have decided the job requires polymeric sand, and end up contemplating a full strip they did not need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case Worth Taking Seriously</h3>

          <p>
            Empty joints in a sealed driveway are the situation that genuinely justifies the hard route. Joints that have lost their sand entirely allow the pavers to shift under vehicle loads, and shifting pavers become a base problem rather than a joint problem. That is worth stripping and re-sanding properly rather than patching with a stabiliser that will not hold under that load.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the Pavers Before Sanding the Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer runs down into the joints and coats the vertical faces that polymeric sand needs to grip, leaving a plug sitting between two slippery walls with no mechanical hold. The joints fail early, and the only proper fix is stripping the sealer and starting the whole sequence again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Another Coat of Sealer to Fix It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On discovering the joints will not hold, the instinct is to seal again and lock everything down. The original sealer is still on the joint walls still preventing the bond, so an extra coat changes nothing underneath while adding film thickness on top that brings its own problems.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing Polymeric Sand Into Already-Sealed Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the patio is already sealed and the joints have eroded, polymeric sand will not bond to the coated walls. Either strip the sealer, clean the joints out thoroughly to expose bare faces, or use a purpose-made joint stabilising sealer designed for exactly this situation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding and Sealing in the Same Week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Getting the order right and the interval wrong produces the other classic failure. Polymeric sand releases moisture for weeks after it feels hard, and sealing at day three or day ten traps it under the film as a milky haze. Allow about 30 days between the two operations.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing the Patio and Sealing the Same Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pre-sealing clean puts water straight back into joints you waited a month to dry. Give it a full dry day afterwards, longer in cool or humid weather, or the careful wait is undone in a morning and produces exactly the trapped-moisture haze it was meant to prevent.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you seal pavers before or after polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After. Polymeric sand needs to bond to the bare, slightly porous vertical faces of the pavers, and a sealer applied first coats those faces with a film that prevents adhesion. Install the sand, allow about 30 days for it to cure fully, then seal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you seal pavers before adding polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The sealer coats the joint walls, so the cured sand sits between two slippery faces with no mechanical grip and lifts out as a strip under traffic and water. Adding another coat of sealer does not help, because the original film is still on the joint walls. Fixing it means stripping and starting again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you add polymeric sand to already sealed pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not effectively without preparation. Either strip the sealer and install properly, clean the joints out thoroughly to expose bare faces on the joint walls, or use a joint stabilising sealer, which is formulated to penetrate and bind loose joint sand rather than seal it off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a joint stabilising sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A sealer formulated to soak into joint sand and bind it in place rather than form a film that excludes it. It is designed for exactly the situation of loose or eroded sand in an already-sealed patio. It is not as strong as properly installed polymeric sand but requires no stripping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the type of sealer change the answer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It changes the severity, not the answer. Film-forming gloss and wet-look sealers are the worst case, coating joint walls with a closed film. Penetrating sealers line the pore structure rather than forming a surface film, so some grip may remain — but sand still belongs first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait between sanding and sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 30 days, and longer in cool, humid or heavily shaded conditions. Polymeric sand is walkable within a day but continues releasing moisture internally for weeks, and a sealer applied before it finishes traps that moisture and produces a milky haze across the pavers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a joint stabilising sealer instead of polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For light-duty situations, yes. Joint stabilising sealers penetrate loose joint sand and bind it in place, and they are the practical answer for an already-sealed patio with slightly eroded joints. They are not as strong as properly installed polymeric sand, so they are not the right choice for empty joints in a driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

