        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sand first, then sealer. Polymeric sand holds by gripping the bare vertical faces of the pavers either side of the joint, and a sealer applied beforehand coats exactly those faces with a film whose whole purpose is to stop things adhering. Get the order wrong and the joints have nothing to hold onto.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            It is a costly error to reverse, which is why it is worth being firm about. Adding another coat of sealer does not fix it, because the original film is still on the joint walls. The genuine remedy is chemically stripping the sealer, cleaning, drying and running the sequence again — a significant job with real risk to the paver faces.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are in the common situation of an already-sealed patio with eroded joints, you have better options than forcing sand into coated joints. Clean the joints out thoroughly to expose bare walls, or use a joint stabilising sealer designed to penetrate and bind loose sand. And whichever route you take, leave about 30 days between sanding and sealing so the joints have finished releasing moisture before a film goes over them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When in doubt about which situation you are in, look at the joints rather than the surface. Eroded but present sand points to the easy route; empty joints point to the thorough one.
          </p>
        </div>

